A major limitation of building statistical dialogue systems is the scarcity of relevant dialogue data. This short paper presents a novel methodology to incorporate external dialogue data that is structurally similar to the desired dialogue dataset, provided that the relevant dialogue characteristics can be formally defined. We focus on building statistical dialogue models for specific domains lacking substantial dialogue data, such as user interface and social fields. After establishing a unified representation of dialogue graphs through node and edge attributes, we devised a clustering method in both node and graph domains to obtain the structural equivalence needed for model integration. We successfully applied this approach to combine the strengths of tiny and large dialogue data to improve the conversation model's performance by 2.5 F1-score points. Moreover, our model can be used for structure-aware pre-training of dialogue system components, such as goal management and response generation. The implementation of our method can be found at https://github.com/sdrl-ab/deep-domain-coupling. Received funding for this work from a 2020 Facebook Research Fellowship awarded to its primary author.}, year = {2021 } } ``` `}
